WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.215
Module Seven, Lesson Two: putting the stack together.

2
00:00:03.545 --> 00:00:06.759
The exam loves end-to-end designs, so let's build one.

3
00:00:07.559 --> 00:00:12.352
Picture a hospital network whose nurses ask an assistant about clinical guidelines.

4
00:00:12.682 --> 00:00:16.856
The first draft, one big model on one GPU, aces the pilot.

5
00:00:17.656 --> 00:00:22.235
Then three hundred nurses log on at once, and the GPU runs out of memory.

6
00:00:22.565 --> 00:00:25.139
And patient data must never leave the hospital.

7
00:00:25.939 --> 00:00:29.367
The rebuild starts with a stateless agent service on Kubernetes.

8
00:00:29.697 --> 00:00:32.249
It scales out on the number of in-flight requests.

9
00:00:33.049 --> 00:00:38.013
Inside, a NeMo Agent Toolkit workflow runs the loop, with profiling and tracing.

10
00:00:38.343 --> 00:00:43.242
NeMo Guardrails adds input, dialog, retrieval, execution and output rails.

11
00:00:44.042 --> 00:00:48.899
The agent calls an LLM NIM that scales on queue depth, with two warm copies.

12
00:00:49.229 --> 00:00:54.064
Embedding and reranking NIMs from NeMo Retriever feed a Milvus vector database.

13
00:00:54.864 --> 00:00:58.334
Tools come through MCP servers and internal APIs.

14
00:00:58.664 --> 00:01:03.457
Postgres and Redis hold state; OpenTelemetry traces feed observability.

15
00:01:04.257 --> 00:01:08.644
Offline, production traces flow through Curator, Customizer and Evaluator.

16
00:01:08.974 --> 00:01:12.572
A new model then moves from shadow, to canary, to full rollout.

17
00:01:13.372 --> 00:01:17.845
Now hardware: does the model fit in one GPU at your chosen precision?

18
00:01:18.175 --> 00:01:21.175
Count weights plus KV cache for your target concurrency.

19
00:01:21.975 --> 00:01:25.147
If not, NVLink makes tensor parallelism worthwhile.

20
00:01:25.477 --> 00:01:30.376
And many small models can share fewer GPUs, with hard isolation, using MIG.

21
00:01:31.176 --> 00:01:34.646
That's where the first draft went wrong: it was sized for weights alone.

22
00:01:34.976 --> 00:01:40.131
With long agent runs at high concurrency, the KV cache can rival or exceed them.

23
00:01:40.931 --> 00:01:44.401
When data must never leave, the entire stack is self-hosted.

24
00:01:44.731 --> 00:01:48.584
NIM containers come from NGC into a private registry;

25
00:01:48.634 --> 00:01:51.529
the NIM Operator caches models on a PVC.

26
00:01:52.329 --> 00:01:57.101
The vector database is self-hosted too, and no tool calls an external API.

27
00:01:57.431 --> 00:01:59.898
This is one of NIM's main reasons to exist.

28
00:02:00.698 --> 00:02:05.270
Exam tip: air-gapped framing recurs in regulated-industry questions,

29
00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:08.328
and capacity plans must include the KV cache.

30
00:02:08.658 --> 00:02:10.464
Read on for the full component map.
